<attribute name="briefDescription" nodeId="93d946b3"></attribute>
<attribute name="suppressed" nodeId="d4b2d501">false</attribute>
<attribute name="orderingGuide" nodeId="c80ba783"></attribute>
<attribute name="authors" nodeId="d1501d52"></attribute>
<attribute name="changeDate" nodeId="ec3fed9e"></attribute>
<attribute name="changeDescription" nodeId="5dc03201"></attribute>
<attribute name="version" nodeId="9e54ee3f"></attribute>
<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="3ae0c051"></attribute>
<attribute name="externalId" nodeId="ffa32cd5"></attribute>
<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="10f79310">&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Structure the meeting carefully so it flows well, starting with a clear opening through to the definition and
        recording of actions at the end.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Use engaging and fun activities throughout the session to keep the energy level and engagement of the participants
        high.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Vary activities from one retrospective to the next so these regular meetings stay fresh and continue to challenge
        the attendees to question their perspectives.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Be tenacious when finalizing the list of&amp;nbsp;actionable tasks at the end of the meeting so that it is small and
        concrete. The agreed upon actions are the true value of the meeting as they will reflect the necessary adaptations
        the team will need to make in order to improve.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Be sure to help the team follow through on the agreed actions; no matter how good the plan is it will not bring any
        value if it is not enacted.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Hold retrospectives at least once per sprint, more if deemed necessary by the Scrum Master and Product Owner.
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="longPresentationName" nodeId="7d2b01c5"></attribute>
<attribute name="usageGuidance" nodeId="805ca1f4"></attribute>
<attribute name="refinedDescription" nodeId="49e77ee6">&lt;p&gt;
    Review and understand, at least once per sprint:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        How the team is performing work
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        What the interactions are between the team and stakeholders
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        What is impacting the team&apos;s ability to perform work
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        What is the impact of people, processes, tools and technologies on the project
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    In order to perform the sprint retrospective, make sure to:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Decide on a theme&lt;/strong&gt; - Select a point of focus for the meeting. For example, if anything severely
        impacted the team&apos;s ability to complete work during the sprint this should be highlighted as a key topic of
        discussion, or theme, for the retrospective.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Agree on a date, time and participants&lt;/strong&gt; - Typically the meeting is held after the sprint review
        meeting, toward the end of the sprint, with the entire team.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Select a facilitator&lt;/strong&gt; - Most commonly, the Scrum Master facilitates the retrospective. However, the
        Scrum Master may opt to participate instead so as to share insights and allow another impartial Scrum Master to
        facilitate.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Send invitations, book a&amp;nbsp;room and secure materials&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;- The retrospective
        leader/facilitator should send invitations to all participants so the meeting starts on time. Next, secure an
        appropriate area to&amp;nbsp;perform the retrospective. Select a venue that is relatively quiet and comfortable to
        support openness and sharing. Finally, check that any necessary supplies (i.e., flipcharts, post-its, markers,
        whiteboards) will be available for use during the meeting if needed.
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
</Element>
